<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lalbagh Botanical Garden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1760</span> · Also known as Lal Bagh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lalbagh Botanical Garden is a historic garden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a> (Bangalore), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> around 1760 on the Mughal garden model and enlarged by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, who brought in plants from many lands. Under British rule it became a government botanical garden, and its Victorian glasshouse, built in the late nineteenth century, hosts the city's flower shows.</p></div>
